{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-177693",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms ${a}_{7}=-39$ and ${a}_{8}=-31$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-31-(-39) \\\\  & =-31+39 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{7}=-39,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{7}$ is $6$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{7} & ={a}_{1}+6d \\\\ -39 & ={a}_{1}+6(8) \\\\ -39 & ={a}_{1}+48 \\\\ -39-48 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-87.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-87+(n-1)(8) \\\\  & =-87+8n-8 \\\\  & =8n-95.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
